WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.438
Imagine thirty parallel worlds, each living through the same hundred years of

00:04.538 --> 00:07.856
an economy. They share the rules, but not the luck. We are

00:07.956 --> 00:11.334
allowed to watch exactly one of them, the red one, and from

00:11.434 --> 00:13.927
it alone we must learn about all the others.

00:15.000 --> 00:17.690
When can one life stand in for many worlds?

00:17.790 --> 00:21.064
That is the question of stationarity and ergodicity.

00:22.133 --> 00:25.839
Freeze the clock at one date and look down the column.

00:25.939 --> 00:29.504
Across the many worlds, the value at that date has a

00:29.604 --> 00:33.592
distribution, and its centre is the expectation, E of x t.

00:33.692 --> 00:36.975
It is an average across worlds, not across time.

00:38.066 --> 00:44.650
Now let the date slide. The column histogram barely moves. That is stationarity:

00:44.750 --> 00:49.329
the law of the process does not depend on when you look.

00:50.400 --> 00:54.809
Compare a random walk, where every shock is remembered forever. Slide

00:54.909 --> 00:58.535
a window along time and collect what the worlds do inside

00:58.635 --> 01:02.456
it. The histogram spreads wider and wider. The variance is t

01:02.556 --> 01:06.835
sigma squared: the date matters, so this process is not stationary.

01:07.933 --> 01:12.087
Back to the stationary world, and now follow a single life for

01:12.187 --> 01:16.135
a long time. Its shocks are remembered for a while and then

01:16.235 --> 01:21.006
forgotten. Every value it visits drops into a histogram. In yellow, the

01:21.106 --> 01:24.368
outline of what the many worlds show at one date.

01:24.466 --> 01:28.629
The one life fills the outline of the many worlds. That is

01:28.729 --> 01:34.877
ergodicity: because memory fades, a single history eventually visits every region the

01:34.977 --> 01:40.096
ensemble occupies, and as often. So the average along time converges to

01:40.196 --> 01:44.507
the average across worlds. That is the law of large numbers.

01:45.600 --> 01:49.723
Now let us break it. At the beginning of time a coin is

01:49.823 --> 01:55.713
tossed, once. Heads, the world lives around plus two; tails, around minus two.

01:57.500 --> 02:01.057
Every date shows the same two humps, so the process is stationary.

02:01.200 --> 02:04.785
But the coin is never tossed again. Our one life stays in

02:04.885 --> 02:09.052
its world forever. Its running average settles on plus two, not on

02:09.152 --> 02:13.643
zero, and no amount of patience reveals the other hump. Stationary, but

02:13.743 --> 02:16.747
not ergodic: the memory of the coin never fades.

02:17.833 --> 02:22.633
So two conditions let one history speak for all. Stationarity:

02:22.733 --> 02:27.297
every date obeys the same law. Ergodicity: memory fades, so

02:27.397 --> 02:31.644
one long life sees everything the many worlds can show.

02:31.744 --> 02:36.071
Together, the time average converges to the expectation.
